Hometown packages (tools/hometown, cities/pkg)
----------------------------------------------
  A Hometown package (?city=pkg&pkg=<id>) is a place compiled by tools/hometown/compile.py from open map data. The
  compiler is part of this repository (MIT); the packages it writes are derived databases and keep their sources'
  terms. Each package's manifest.json lists its sources, their versions and these credits:
  - (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, Open Database License 1.0 (https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright): the road
    network (names, classes, one-ways), water and land use, and the OpenStreetMap-derived building footprints, read
    through Overture Maps. A package's geodata.js is an ODbL derived database.
  - Overture Maps Foundation (https://overturemaps.org), licence by theme (https://docs.overturemaps.org/attribution/):
    buildings (footprints and heights) ODbL 1.0, with rows from OpenStreetMap, Microsoft's ML building footprints
    (ODbL) and Esri Community Maps contributors (CC BY 4.0); base (land cover, water) ODbL 1.0; places CDLA
    Permissive 2.0.
  - USGS 3D Elevation Program, 1/3 arc-second DEM: US government work, public domain.
  - tools/hometown/osm/osm-roads-us.parquet (the compiler's lane side table: lanes, widths, one-ways, speed limits
    by OSM way id): (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, Open Database License 1.0, a derived database made from Geofabrik's
    US extract by tools/hometown/osm_roads.py. NOT covered by the MIT License in LICENSE. See tools/hometown/osm/NOTICE.md.
  No brand name is shown: businesses are named by what they are and the street they stand on.

  Where the game credits the map data (and why there is no line over the play)
    The credit, "© OpenStreetMap contributors · Overture Maps · USGS 3DEP" (the short line, from the package's
    attribution lines, cities/pkg/js/pkg-hud.js pkgCredit), is shown:
      - on the loading screen, linked to https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright (pkg-hud.js, #pkg-load-credit);
      - in the start gate's small print, the title screen a story world opens on (wagmi/storyrun/index.js);
      - at the foot of the pause menu, linked (wagmi/mission/pause.js), and first on its Credits page with every
        source's licence and link (wagmi/credits.js);
      - in the corner of the full map, the one place the data is shown as a map (pkg-hud.js, .mc-map-credit);
      - and on GameMog's library page.
    It is not drawn over the game view while playing. Reasoning:
      - ODbL 1.0 s.4.3 asks a Produced Work's notice to be "reasonably calculated" to make anyone using or viewing
        it aware the data is from OpenStreetMap and available under ODbL. The OSMF Attribution Guidelines (2021)
        say how, by medium. For "Computer games and simulations" attribution may be given by a splash screen at
        startup, in the game view, on the credits page, in a menu or another suitable place; a short credit
        needs the details somewhere suitable (the Credits page, the copyright link). A splash credit must be
        legible and shown long enough to read (the loading screen and the start gate are up for seconds and
        wait for the player). The "corner of the map" rule is for interactive maps: here the full map.
      - Overture: its ODbL themes carry OpenStreetMap's obligation above (and the Microsoft rows' ODbL, the same).
        The Esri Community Maps rows are CC BY 4.0, which takes a credit "in any reasonable manner based on the
        medium": the Credits page names them. CDLA Permissive 2.0 (places) puts no condition on Results (s.3.1),
        and no on-screen credit anywhere; when the Data itself is shared it asks for the agreement's text with it
        (s.2.1): a package's manifest names the licence, and its text is at https://cdla.dev/permissive-2-0/. Overture asks
        for "Overture Maps Foundation" to be credited: it is, in the short line.
      - USGS 3DEP is a US government work in the public domain: no credit is required; USGS asks for one as a
        courtesy, and it is given.
    Revisit this if a package is ever offered for download as data (then ODbL s.4.4 / 4.6 and CDLA s.2.1 apply to
    the file itself), or if the game ever shows the town as a map outside the full map (then that map's corner).

